# Feature Workflow — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Run the steps in order; never skip the real check, the KB sync or the change-log entry.

> Golden rules: research before coding · ground in the KB · tests ship with the change · nothing is done
> until it has been run for real · the KB and the change log ship with the change · push once ·
> the developer merges · never touch secrets.

## 0. Sync with the remote
`git fetch origin`, read `git log --oneline HEAD..origin/{{BASE_BRANCH}}`, merge it in. Repeat before you
believe a gate and before you push (`.claude/rules/git.md`).

## 1. Research (first)
For a new module or anything unclear, run the **`deep-research`** skill before any code; it writes a dossier
to `.claude/knowledgebase/research/<module>/research.md`. For a small change, the **context-gatherer** agent
is enough. Read the relevant KB docs, `.claude/database/database.md` and the code you'll touch; reuse what exists.

## 2. Capture and decide
Write the ticket's plan file `.claude/plans/inprogress/<TICKET>.md` from `.claude/plans/TEMPLATE.md`: problem,
scope, decisions, and for any change to a RULE (who/when/money/dates/permissions/notifications) **the case
table first** (situation → expected result → the test that pins it). Resolve ambiguities yourself and record
the assumption; ask the owner only for a genuine business decision.

## 3. Branch
From `{{BASE_BRANCH}}`: `feat/<short-name>` or `fix/<short>`.

## Briefing a subagent (every spawn)
A brief stands on its own: the task, the worktree path, the plan file, and **the list of files the agent may
edit** ("edit only these; if another file needs changing, stop and report it"). A read-only agent is told it
changes no files and runs no state-changing git (`stash`, `reset`, `checkout`, `switch`, `restore`, `clean`,
`commit`). Name the plan file instead of pasting history.

## 4. Implement (small slices)
{{Project structure rule — thin routes/UI, logic in the right layer. New data → migration with
access control in the same change.}} The smallest correct change; match existing patterns.

## 5. Test first, then the gate
Write or update the test for the changed behaviour BEFORE finishing the slice, see it fail once (break the
code a moment), then the gate: {{GATE}} (type-check → lint → tests → build). Loop until green.

## 6. Reviews — off by default
No reviewer agents and no PR-review cycle before a merge unless the developer asks (then: **security-reviewer**
and **quality-reviewer** in parallel; **professional-qa** for milestones, via the `qa-review` skill). The
builder's own discipline replaces them: the case table, the test seen failing, the gate, a read of its own diff.

## 7. Verify for real
{{How to actually exercise the change: drive the UI in a real browser and read the screenshot / hit the API and
read the response.}} Until then the report is "implemented and green", never "done".

## 8. Sync the knowledge base and log it
Update the module doc under `.claude/knowledgebase/`, `.claude/database/database.md` on a schema change,
`docs/SETUP.md` for anything a new machine needs, `CARRY-OVER.md` for follow-ups, and write the
`07-change-log.md` entry (`.claude/rules/change-log.md`) — in the SAME change as the code.

## 9. Push once and open the PR
Commit per slice (`Co-Authored-By:` trailer). Push **once**, when the feature is ready and green. Open the PR
into `{{BASE_BRANCH}}` with what changed, why, and how it was verified.

## 10. Merge and deploy
Merge the PR into `{{BASE_BRANCH}}` once the gate is green on the head merged with it, then deploy the
non-production environments and check the deploy. A production merge or release waits for the developer's
go. On merge the plan file moves to `.claude/plans/done/` with an Outcome. The `pr-review-cycle` skill runs
only when the developer asks for a review.

